  "prediction": "**Executive Discretion and Emergency Powers: A Review of the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA)**\n\nThe International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) has been a cornerstone of the US government's ability to respond to national emergencies and threats to national security. Enacted in 1977, IEEPA grants the President broad authority to regulate economic transactions and impose sanctions on foreign entities in response to an \"unusual and extraordinary threat\" to the US national security, foreign policy, or economy.\n\nThe report examines the history of IEEPA and its predecessor, the Trading with the Enemy Act (TWEA), which was enacted during World War I. TWEA granted the President extensive powers to regulate international trade and commerce, and its provisions were later expanded to include peacetime emergencies. The report notes that the use of emergency powers has expanded over time, with Presidents declaring national emergencies and renewing them for years or even decades.\n\nThe report highlights several criticisms of IEEPA, including the lack of definition of key terms such as \"national emergency\" and \"unusual and extraordinary threat.\" The scope of presidential authority under IEEPA has also become less constrained in a highly globalized era, and the use of IEEPA has led to concerns about the erosion of congressional authority.\n\nThe report also examines the use of IEEPA in recent years, including the imposition of sanctions on foreign entities and the control of exports. The report notes that the use of IEEPA has been largely uncontroversial, with Congress often approving explicitly of the President's use of the statute.\n\nIn light of these concerns, the report recommends several potential reforms to IEEPA, including:\n\n1. Defining key terms such as \"national emergency\" and \"unusual and extraordinary threat.\"\n2. Establishing a review mechanism to give Congress an active role in overseeing the use of IEEPA.\n3. Amending IEEPA to include a \"sunset provision\" terminating any national emergency after a certain number of days.\n4. Providing for congressional approval of national emergency declarations through a joint resolution.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of ensuring that the use of emergency powers is subject to adequate congressional oversight and that the President's authority is not unchecked.",
